WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.750
Film Courage: Did anyone try to talk you out of making
this film? Mark Landsman, Filmmaker of SCANDALOUS: The Untold Story of the National Enquirer: My accountant.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:12.420
Film Courage: Okay aside from your accountant? Mark: No. Film Courage:
No one said "don't go down this rabbit

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.279
hole?"  Mark: The opposite, the
opposite. You know it's like some things

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:23.460
are kind of self-evident. I
think for me I'm interested in subjects

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.240
that are irrelevant to our world that
are happening now that have an impact on

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.759
on all of us.
So I was seeing this having a tremendous

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:37.649
impact plus I'm really interested in the
way that the media impacts our lives

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:41.280
I've always been really fascinated with
that. Film Courage: Do you think you could have worked

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.460
for the National Enquirer say 1980s
1990s? Mark: So funny when the film premiered

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:54.690
in LA a few weeks ago a couple of these
Enquirer reporters were there on stage

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.260
with me and one of them we know were and
said you know mark would have made an

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.370
amazing Inquirer reporter and Amy
Nicholson knew who is our moderator I

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.610
mean and she said so Mark would you have
would you have been interested in being

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:11.580
an inquiry reporter? And the answer is no.
I wouldn't have I think that it's a real

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:19.229
Faustian bargain to take that job you
are trading a lot of your own ethics

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:25.890
really for that job
I like aspects of what they did I

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:32.850
I really believe in holding truth to
power I really believe that people who

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:39.060
do things that are unethical
who are in the public eye should should

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.500
be exposed I think that I don't have any
problem with that

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.820
so some of the things they did I think
were great in terms of that certainly

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.590
the reporting they did in the OJ case
the John Edwards situation. Those were

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:59.700
things that I think were in the realm of
I'd say good good reporting but for the

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:05.579
most part it's just been i think
something that has helped to create

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:11.100
journalism and it
the public's understanding of what is a

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.480
fact and what is not a fact and that's
dangerous so no I wouldn't have been

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.640
interested in being a part of that. Film Courage: Had
you heard the term 'catch and kill' before?

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:25.020
Mark: Before we started the film actually
working on the film in earnest I hadn't

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.750
heard the term 'catch and kill' because
the first time I heard the term catch

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:35.130
and kill was when I read Ronan Farrow's
article in it was April of 2018 about

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:40.140
Karen McDougal and that was when that
was the first time that I'd heard about

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:45.150
it that's the first time that I think it
started to get introduced into the news

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.860
media in a big way in making the film
before that like we were shooting before

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.360
that all came out which was wild that
was kind of freakish that we were out in

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:00.150
the world filming The Enquirer and then
this big revelation called 'catch and

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.600
kill' happens that the timing of that was
really unusual but what we were learning

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.900
on the field from these old-time
Enquirer reporters like no we never

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.870
heard of the 
term catch and kill before we always

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:14.790
called it trade outs like okay we have
some really sketchy information about

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:20.940
this celebrity you know Bob Hope for
example and so we're gonna present that

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.230
information to Mr. Hope's team, his
publicist, his lawyer his agent whatever

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:30.420
it is and then in exchange for not
running that story he's going to...

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:35.550
they're gonna trade out for a story
about Christmas at home with the hopes

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:41.100
or a ride on the golf course with you
know and they did this for all of the

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:47.060
major celebrities so you know you can
imagine how much dirt they acquired

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.150
decade after decade of doing that kind
of a practice but they called it trade

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.390
outs they didn't call it catch and kill
catch and kill was something that I

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.560
first heard of through Ronan Farrow's
reporting and then they were also by the

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.000
way his book is fantastic okay yeah and
you have him in the film as well briefly

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.040
okay yeah our kind of job and then there
were also where they would purchase the

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:13.110
rights to a story and sit on it
was that like another form of catching

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.459
kill innocence because then that story
sort of was taken out of the hands of

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:23.020
individual yep that happened in in a
number of instances and in the film

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:27.340
there's there's a number of anecdotes
that that go into that and that's a very

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.480
disturbing practice because a celebrity
has been caught in the act of doing

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:38.830
something really heinous and often times
it was celebrities who would go on to do

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.420
things much worse down the line and so
you have to wonder had those stories

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:48.610
come to light back then and not been
buried would the with the behavior that

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:52.630
happened later have happened and that's
that's where I think the story gets

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.570
really chilling does the public have a
right to know everything about

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:58.690
celebrities in your opinion after having
done this film you know there's like

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.460
several camps on that some people think
once you get into the public eye your

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:08.350
life your secrets are no longer your own
the public owns you in some sense I

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:12.790
don't believe that I don't believe that
I mean you know why should that be the

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:18.460
case I I think that a lot of a lot of
gossip reporters a lot of tabloid

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.330
reporters use that I think to go to
sleep at night and look at themselves in

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:27.850
the mirror but I don't believe that just
because somebody is entertaining us or

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:34.120
compelling us with a great performance
or you know engaging us with incredible

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.510
music or whatever it is that they're
doing that we're entitled to know

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:42.520
everything about them I think that
that's I just don't think that that's

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:50.590
right and I think that the truth is is
that it's a business and that there are

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:58.360
benefits that celebrities and creative
professionals artists musicians get from

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.290
being in tabloids they get publicity
their permit they get to Brod films

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:08.920
people buy into their image they buy
into the narrative but I think this kind

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:14.950
of ruthless pursuit of people you know
hounding them with paparazzi hounding

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:20.490
their children I don't I don't see the
point I think that's just more about a

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:24.640
sickness in our culture you know this
obsession with wanting to know

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.030
everything about other people this
comparison compare and despair

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:34.970
you know and that's that's that's a
larger issue well one part that was

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:39.080
especially chilling in the film for me
was when one of the reporters or maybe

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:43.310
two talked about how it was actual
family members not not just disgruntled

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:47.930
employees of a celebrity or a politician
that called in tips but actually like

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:52.250
family members and supposed friends and
it was really based on jealousy

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:57.620
I was just that was that was sort of
like a real gut reaction and and just I

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:00.920
don't know what are your thoughts on
that I miss you far I was fascinated

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:06.530
with how many people would sell would
sell people out you know that was people

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:09.620
ask me like what's the what are some of
the most interesting things that you

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:14.360
discovered in making the film one of the
most interesting things was the lengths

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:18.980
to which these Enquirer reporters would
go to get sources around famous people

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:27.380
so like nurses who were checking people
into hospitals, valets, maitre d's at

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:32.840
restaurants, waiters at restaurants,
barbers, beauticians, but also close

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:38.300
family members. You know disgruntled
lovers, the sister that is the

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:43.220
black sheep of the family, a child
whatever. It is all these people were on

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:49.880
the Enquirer payroll so you know I don't
think it's any different today but it is

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:53.300
pretty chilling when you realize that
you know you get famous you get rich you

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:59.690
get powerful and there is a price to pay
for that and then you become susceptible

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:04.370
to people selling you out.
Film Courage: Was there one thing that was a huge

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:09.710
eye-opener for you in doing this film?
Mark: I think you know what's so what's so

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:18.580
interesting the I think the the the
number of people involved in gathering

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:24.100
negative stories are up about the rich
the famous and the powerful and then

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:29.150
brokering those stories to curry favor
or whatever they're doing whatever

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:35.690
reason it is why they would take a
negative story and keep it and then you

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:40.550
know that that network was really
eye-opening the fact that that exists

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:45.389
and not just in the Inquirer
on a much broader level you know

00:08:45.389 --> 00:08:50.069
somebody in our film says you know that
there are thousands of these deals made

00:08:50.069 --> 00:08:57.089
every day to protect the rich and
powerful that's that's pretty

00:08:57.089 --> 00:09:00.800
eye-opening that's a very sobering
thought you know that if you have money

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:06.930
and you are in a position of power in
the country and the culture that you can

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:11.790
use those resources to maintain a bright
and shiny image and I think making this

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:19.319
film made all of us very aware of just
how often that happens. Film Courage: Sure...wasn't Orson

